# Sens_main_real.mac: GATE 8.2 model of the Bruker Si 198 PET insert in the 9.4 T MR scanner, for the
# sensitivity test of NEMA NU 4-2008 (section 5).
# A Na-22 point source in a 10 mm acrylic cube sits on the scanner axis and is stepped along z.
# The RF coil and the animal cradle are real parts, cut at z = +-80 mm (files in coil_bed_real/).
# The file holds three coil/cradle setups (see SETUP below). Exactly one of them is active:
#   q86    : Quad86 coil + mouse cradle, 127,075 Bq (Quad86 sheet of the measured workbook)
#   q35    : Quad35 coil + mouse cradle, 131,126 Bq (Quad35 sheet)
#   q86rat : Quad86 coil + rat cradle,   127,075 Bq (Quad86 sheet)
# Keep coil_bed_real/ and Material/ next to this file. I run it with sens_run.py, which activates the chosen
# setup and writes one macro per source position, for example:
#   python3 sens_run.py --setup q35 --tag NAME --positions-xlsx SensPostionsQuad35.xlsx --no-lu176
# sens_analysis.py then applies the NEMA analysis (section 5.4) to the ROOT files.

########################## SCANNER ##########################
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# Bruker Si 198 PET insert
# Axial FOV: 150 mm
# Inner diameter of the air system envelope (PET bore): 113.8 mm
# Detector material: LYSO, one monolithic block per detector
# Block shape: trapezoidal (50 x 50 mm exit face, 48 x 48 mm entrance face, 10 mm thick)
# Detectors per ring: 8
# Detector rings: 3 (ring pitch 52 mm)
# Ring diameter: 117 mm between opposite entrance faces
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

# Energy window 255.5-766.5 keV, the 50 % window of the sensitivity measurement (Kuntner et al. 2025).
# The nearest crystal entrance faces are at r 58.5 mm (117 mm face to face, the ring diameter in the header
# of Bruker's list-mode files).

#    exit face
# ---------------- 
# \			  /   Trapezoidal shape of the crystal
#  --------------  
#   entrance face



######################## RUN SETTINGS #######################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The values that change from one source position to the next. sens_run.py writes one copy of this file per
# position and changes only these lines (and the SETUP block below). Run directly, this file simulates the
# source at the centre of the FOV.
# In the measurement (Kuntner et al. 2025; 2021-2022_NEMA_Sensitivity_Masar.xlsx) the source was on the axis,
# stepped in 1-2 mm steps along the 150 mm axial FOV, 60 s per position.
# No comments on the alias lines: an alias takes the rest of its line as its value.
#   sourceX, sourceY, sourceZ : source position in mm. On the axis X = Y = 0; Z = axial position
#                               (0 = centre of the axial FOV, +-75 = its ends)
#   duration                  : acquisition time per position, s
#   seed                      : random seed, different for every position
#   output                    : ROOT file name without .root

/control/alias sourceX 0
/control/alias sourceY 0
/control/alias sourceZ 0
/control/alias duration 60
/control/alias seed 123456789
/control/alias output output/Sens_z0



########################## SETUP (coil and bed) ########################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# Three setups. Exactly one block is active; the alias lines of the other two are commented out.
# sens_run.py --setup q86 | q35 | q86rat activates the chosen block in the macros it writes. Run directly
# with Gate, this file uses the block that is active here. To switch by hand, remove the '#' in front of
# the four alias lines of one block and put it in front of the alias lines of the other two blocks.
# The '#@setup' and '#@end' lines mark the blocks for sens_run.py: keep them.
# No comments on the alias lines: an alias takes the rest of its line as its value.
#   activity  : Na-22 activity in Bq (cell D2 of the measured sheet for that coil)
#   coilMacro : the coil (coil_bed_real/, fixed, G10 cylinders on the axis, z -80 ... +80 mm)
#   bedMacro  : the cradle (coil_bed_real/, moves with the source, see CRADLE below)
#   cradleY   : cradle height in mm (see CRADLE below)

#@setup q86 sheet=Quad86 positions=SensPostionsQuad86.xlsx
# Quad86 coil + mouse cradle. The cube sits 0.01 mm above the cradle floor.
# The cradle reaches r 17.98 mm.
/control/alias activity 127075
/control/alias coilMacro coil_bed_real/quad86_coil_cut80_uniform.mac
/control/alias bedMacro coil_bed_real/mouse_cradle3mm_cut80_sens.mac
/control/alias cradleY 7.045
#@end

#@setup q35 sheet=Quad35 positions=SensPostionsQuad35.xlsx
# Quad35 coil + mouse cradle, raised as far as it fits in the coil.
# The cradle reaches r 17.45 mm (coil wall at 17.5 mm).
# So the cube sits 1.64 mm above the cradle floor.
# Touching the cube (7.045 mm) would put the cradle 0.48 mm into the coil wall.
#/control/alias activity 131126
#/control/alias coilMacro coil_bed_real/quad35_coil_cut80_uniform.mac
#/control/alias bedMacro coil_bed_real/mouse_cradle3mm_cut80_sens.mac
#/control/alias cradleY 5.411
#@end

#@setup q86rat sheet=Quad86 positions=SensPostionsQuad86.xlsx
# Quad86 coil + rat cradle. The cube sits 0.01 mm above the cradle floor.
# The cradle reaches r 39.14 mm (coil 43.0 mm).
#/control/alias activity 127075
#/control/alias coilMacro coil_bed_real/quad86_coil_cut80_uniform.mac
#/control/alias bedMacro coil_bed_real/rat_cradle_hdpe_cut80_sens.mac
#/control/alias cradleY 19.740
#@end



########################## MATERIALS ########################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The material database of GATE, in the folder Material/.

/gate/geometry/setMaterialDatabase Material/GateMaterials.db



########################## VERBOSITY ########################
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# Verbosity: how much GATE prints to the log while it runs (0 = minimal, higher numbers = more detail).

/gate/verbose Physic    5
/gate/verbose Cuts      0
/gate/verbose SD        0
/gate/verbose Actions   0
/gate/verbose Actor     0
/gate/verbose Step      0
/gate/verbose Error                               1	# show simulation errors
/gate/verbose Warning                             1	# show navigation, physics and sorter warnings
/gate/verbose Output    0
/gate/verbose Volume    0
/gate/verbose Image     0
/gate/verbose Geometry  0
/gate/verbose Core      0

/run/verbose 0
/event/verbose 0 
/tracking/verbose 0



########################## WORLD ########################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

# The world: the room, filled with air, that holds the scanner and all other volumes.

/gate/world/setMaterial Air
/gate/world/geometry/setXLength 100 cm
/gate/world/geometry/setYLength 100 cm
/gate/world/geometry/setZLength 100 cm
/gate/world/vis/setVisible 0



########################## PET MODEL ########################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

### PET SYSTEM ###
# The PET system is a daughter of the world. I use the GATE system PETscanner because it accepts trapezoidal
# crystals. The system name must be written exactly as in the GATE documentation.

/gate/world/daughters/name                 PETscanner	# system name as in the GATE documentation
/gate/world/daughters/insert               cylinder	# cylindrical envelope
/gate/PETscanner/setMaterial               Air		# air
/gate/PETscanner/geometry/setRmin                 56.9 mm	# inner radius = PET bore (113.8 mm); the front pad and the crystals lie outside it
/gate/PETscanner/geometry/setRmax                 99 mm	# outer radius of the air system envelope
/gate/PETscanner/geometry/setHeight               155 mm	# length of the envelope; the three rings span 154 mm at the exit faces
/gate/PETscanner/placement/setTranslation  0 0 0 cm	# centred in the world
/gate/PETscanner/vis/setColor              red		# colour in the viewer
/gate/PETscanner/vis/setVisible            0			# 0 = do not visualise, 1 = visualise
#/gate/PETscanner/vis/forceWireframe


### HEAD ###
# A head is one of the eight detector sectors. It holds the three modules of that sector, their copper
# sheets and the front pad, and it is a daughter of the PETscanner system.
# Each head is a 45 degree air sector, so the heads do not overlap each other or the inner surface of the
# PETscanner volume. Its local origin is the scanner origin, so the translations below are the real
# positions of the parts.

/gate/PETscanner/daughters/name      head
/gate/PETscanner/daughters/insert    cylinder
/gate/head/setMaterial              Air
/gate/head/geometry/setRmin          56.9 mm
/gate/head/geometry/setRmax          75 mm
/gate/head/geometry/setHeight        155 mm
/gate/head/geometry/setPhiStart      67.5 deg
/gate/head/geometry/setDeltaPhi      45 deg
/gate/head/placement/setTranslation  0 0 0 mm
/gate/head/vis/setVisible            0			# 0 = do not visualise, 1 = visualise
#/gate/head/vis/forceWireframe


### MODULE ###
# A module holds one crystal. It is a trapezoid of the same shape as the crystal, so it stays inside its
# 45 degree sector.

/gate/head/daughters/name                module
/gate/head/daughters/insert              trap
/gate/module/setMaterial                Air
/gate/module/geometry/setDx1             25 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDx2             25 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDx3             24 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDx4             24 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDy1             25 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDy2             24 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDz              5 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setTheta           0 deg
/gate/module/geometry/setPhi             0 deg
/gate/module/geometry/setAlp1            0 deg
/gate/module/geometry/setAlp2            0 deg
/gate/module/placement/setTranslation    0 63.5 0 mm	# entrance face at r 58.5 mm: ring diameter 117 mm, as in Bruker's list-mode header
/gate/module/placement/setRotationAxis   1 0 0	# rotate around the x axis only
/gate/module/placement/setRotationAngle  90 deg	# rotate by 90 degrees
/gate/module/vis/setVisible              0		# 0 = do not visualise, 1 = visualise
/gate/module/vis/forceWireframe


### CRYSTAL ###
# The crystal: one monolithic LYSO block per module, trapezoidal, 50 x 50 mm at the exit face, 48 x 48 mm at
# the entrance face, 10 mm thick.
# The LYSO material alone does not produce Lu-176 decays. The intrinsic activity is a separate source
# (see Intrinsic Activity Source below).

/gate/module/daughters/name             crystal	# the name is free in the PETscanner system
/gate/module/daughters/insert           trap		# trapezoid
/gate/crystal/setMaterial               LYSO 	# scintillator, defined in GateMaterials.db
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx1           25 mm	# half width X1 at the exit face (block 50 mm)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx2           25 mm	# half width X2 at the exit face (block 50 mm)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx3           24 mm	# half width X3 at the entrance face (block 48 mm)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx4           24 mm	# half width X4 at the entrance face (block 48 mm)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDy1           25 mm	# half width Y1 at the exit face (block 50 mm)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDy2           24 mm	# half width Y2 at the entrance face (block 48 mm)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDz            5 mm		# half thickness (block 10 mm)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setTheta         0 deg	
/gate/crystal/geometry/setPhi           0 deg
/gate/crystal/geometry/setAlp1          0 deg	
/gate/crystal/geometry/setAlp2          0 deg	
/gate/crystal/placement/setTranslation  0 0 0 mm	# centre of the module
/gate/crystal/vis/setColor              white	# colour in the viewer
/gate/crystal/vis/setVisible            1		# 0 = do not visualise, 1 = visualise
/gate/crystal/vis/forceSolid					# drawn solid in the viewer


### COPPER SHIELDING ###
# A thin copper sheet in front of each block stands for the RF shielding of the insert. It shields against
# RF, not against the static 9.4 T field. Its thickness, 0.0351 mm, is taken from Thiessen et al., which
# describes a different PET insert, so for this scanner it is an assumption. Like every other material in
# the model, the copper attenuates and scatters the photons.

/gate/head/daughters/name                   shielding			# arbitrary name
/gate/head/daughters/insert                 box				# thin sheet
/gate/shielding/setMaterial                 Copper			# see Thiessen et al., https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-9155/61/22/7934
/gate/shielding/geometry/setXLength         48 mm				# X size, as the crystal entrance face
/gate/shielding/geometry/setYLength         48 mm				# Y size, as the crystal entrance face
/gate/shielding/geometry/setZLength         0.0351 mm			# thickness of the copper sheet (Thiessen et al.)
/gate/shielding/placement/setTranslation          0. 57.98145 0. mm	# 0.501 mm in front of the crystal entrance face
/gate/shielding/placement/setRotationAxis   1 0 0				# rotated like the module
/gate/shielding/placement/setRotationAngle  90 deg			# rotate by 90 degrees
/gate/shielding/vis/setColor                red				# colour in the viewer
/gate/shielding/vis/setVisible              1 				# 0 = do not visualise, 1 = visualise
/gate/shielding/vis/forceSolid							# drawn solid in the viewer


### Front Pad ###
# The front pad is the nylon cover on the entrance side of the detectors. The inserted RF coil sits inside it
# without touching the crystals. I model it as one flat plate per head, so eight plates in all.

/gate/head/daughters/name                  FrontPad		# arbitrary name
/gate/head/daughters/insert                box			
/gate/FrontPad/setMaterial                 Nylon			# material of the real pad
/gate/FrontPad/geometry/setXLength         47.00 mm		# width; the eight plates form a faceted ring
/gate/FrontPad/geometry/setYLength         154 mm			# axial length
/gate/FrontPad/geometry/setZLength         1 mm			# thickness
/gate/FrontPad/placement/setTranslation           0. 57.4629 0. mm	# inner face at r 56.963 mm; 0.001 mm gap to the copper sheet
/gate/FrontPad/placement/setRotationAxis   1 0 0			# rotated like the module
/gate/FrontPad/placement/setRotationAngle  90 deg			# rotate by 90 degrees
/gate/FrontPad/vis/setColor                blue			# colour in the viewer
/gate/FrontPad/vis/setVisible              1				# 0 = do not visualise, 1 = visualise
/gate/FrontPad/vis/forceSolid							# drawn solid in the viewer



########################## VOLUMES REPETITION ########################
# Above, one crystal, one copper sheet and one front pad are defined. The repeaters below copy them to make
# the full detector.


### REPEAT CRYSTAL ###
# One crystal per module: no repetition.

/gate/crystal/repeaters/insert             cubicArray		# repeat on a grid
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1				# repeat in the X direction
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1				# repeat in the Y direction
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  1				# repeat in the Z direction
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0.0 0 0.0 mm	# no repetition


### REPEAT MODULE ###
# Three modules along z, one per ring (the module is a daughter of the head).
# The ring pitch is 52 mm, which leaves a 4 mm gap between the rings at the entrance faces (2 mm at the exit
# faces). I chose 52 mm because it reproduces the steps of the measured axial sensitivity profile at the
# ring boundaries; it is not taken from a drawing of the scanner.

/gate/module/repeaters/insert             cubicArray		# repeat on a grid
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1				# repeat in the X direction
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1				# repeat in the Y direction
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  3				# 3 modules along z: the three rings
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0. 0. 52 mm		# ring pitch 52 mm


### REPEAT SHIELDING ###
# Three copper sheets along z, one per ring, with the same pitch as the modules (the sheet is a daughter
# of the head).

/gate/shielding/repeaters/insert             cubicArray	# repeat on a grid
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1			# repeat in the X direction
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1			# repeat in the Y direction
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  3			# 3 sheets along z, one per ring
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0. 0. 52 mm	# same 52 mm pitch as the modules, so each copper sheet stays on its crystal


### REPEAT HEAD ###
# Eight heads around the axis. Each head holds three modules along z, so the scanner has 3 rings of
# 8 blocks (24 blocks in all).

/gate/head/repeaters/insert      ring		# repeat around an axis
/gate/head/ring/setRepeatNumber  8			# 8 heads per ring
/gate/head/ring/setPoint1        0. 0. 1.	# the axis runs through point 1 and point 2: the z axis
/gate/head/ring/setPoint2        0. 0. 0.
/gate/head/ring/enableAutoRotation      # Rotate each sector and its contents around the ring.


### REPEAT FRONTPAD ###
# One front pad per head: no repetition here. The head ring repeats it eight times.

/gate/FrontPad/repeaters/insert             cubicArray		# repeat on a grid
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1			# repeat in the X direction
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1			# repeat in the Y direction
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  1			# repeat in the Z direction
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0. 0. 0. mm	# no repetition


### ATTACH SYSTEM ###
# The volume levels of the PETscanner system, in order: head, module, crystal.

/gate/systems/PETscanner/level1/attach  head
/gate/systems/PETscanner/level2/attach  module
/gate/systems/PETscanner/level3/attach  crystal


### ATTACH CRYSTALSD ###
# The crystals are the sensitive detector (CrystalSD): the detector hits are recorded there.

/gate/crystal/attachCrystalSD


### DESCRIBE SYSTEM ###

/gate/systems/PETscanner/describe



##################### Coil Configuration ####################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The RF coil: {coilMacro} of the active setup, G10 cylinders on the axis with uniform walls, cut at
# z = +-80 mm. The coil is fixed. Its parts attach their own PhantomSD.
# Coil and cradle use the materials of coil_bed_real/Coil3Bed.db (AC_G10 1.79 g/cm3, Bed_HDPE 0.95 g/cm3),
# loaded here in addition to Material/GateMaterials.db. The outer tubes end at r 56.89 mm, inside the
# PETscanner envelope (56.9 mm) and clear of the front pad (56.963 mm).

/gate/geometry/setMaterialDatabase coil_bed_real/Coil3Bed.db

/control/execute {coilMacro}



####################### Magnetic Field ######################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# Static field of the MR scanner: 9.4 T along the scanner axis (z).

/gate/geometry/setMagField  0  0  9.4 T



###################### CRADLE (REAL), MOVES WITH THE SOURCE ####################
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The cradle: {bedMacro} of the active setup. Either the mouse cradle (solid side walls, at least 3 mm thick
# near the floor) or the rat cradle (shape from the attenuation map, mu-map), both in polyethylene
# (Bed_HDPE, 0.95 g/cm3). The material is my assumption: both real cradles are white and opaque.
# In the sensitivity measurement the source cube lay on the mouse cradle.
# Each cradle has 10 parts around the cradle centre, cut at +-80 mm. They are world daughters and attach
# their own PhantomSD.
# The cradle moves with the source: every part is placed at (0, {cradleY}, {sourceZ}), so the source cube
# sits at the centre of the cradle (cradle z = 0) at every axial position.
# The +-80 mm piece is enough for every pair that reaches the crystals: back-to-back photons from a source
# at z both hit a crystal (r >= 58.5 mm, |z| <= 76 mm) only if each travels at most 76 - |z| mm along the axis,
# so they cross the cradle within 24 mm (mouse, r <= 18 mm) or 51 mm (rat, r <= 39.14 mm) of the source.
# cradleY (SETUP) raises the cradle towards the cube, which stays centred on the scanner axis (NEMA). The
# floor is flat under the cube, at y = -12.055 mm (mouse) and -24.750 mm (rat) in the cradle's own frame.

/control/execute {bedMacro}



###################### POINT-SOURCE HOLDER ######################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# NEMA NU 4-2008 section 3.3.3: the point source is embedded in an acrylic cube of 10.0 mm extent on all
# sides (Kuntner et al. 2025: Na-22, 0.3 mm diameter, 10 mm acrylic cube).
# The material 'Plastic' in Material/GateMaterials.db is C5H8O2 (atom counts) at 1.18 g/cm3, that is acrylic
# (PMMA). Do not use the database entry named 'PMMA': it gives the C5H8O2 atom counts as mass fractions
# (53 % hydrogen by mass instead of 8 %).
# Position = {sourceX} {sourceY} {sourceZ} from RUN SETTINGS (sens_run.py: on the axis). The bottom face of
# the cube is 0.01 mm (q86, q86rat) or 1.64 mm (q35) above the cradle floor (cradleY in SETUP; the cradle
# follows sourceZ, not X or Y). Its corners are 7.1 mm from the axis (the coils start at r 17.5 mm).

/gate/world/daughters/name                   sourceCube	# arbitrary name
/gate/world/daughters/insert                 box		# box
/gate/sourceCube/setMaterial                 Plastic		# acrylic (C5H8O2, 1.18 g/cm3)
/gate/sourceCube/geometry/setXLength         10. mm		# NEMA: 10 mm on all sides
/gate/sourceCube/geometry/setYLength         10. mm
/gate/sourceCube/geometry/setZLength         10. mm
/gate/sourceCube/placement/setTranslation    {sourceX} {sourceY} {sourceZ} mm	# set in RUN SETTINGS
/gate/sourceCube/vis/setColor                gray
/gate/sourceCube/vis/setVisible              1
/gate/sourceCube/vis/forceSolid

####################### ATTACH PhantomSD ####################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# PhantomSD records the Compton and Rayleigh scatter of each photon in these passive volumes, so the ROOT
# file tells scattered from unscattered pairs. Leaving a volume out does not stop the scatter in it; it only
# hides that scatter from these labels. So every passive volume is attached.
# Scatter labelled this way is not the NEMA scatter fraction, which needs the NEMA projection analysis.

/gate/FrontPad/attachPhantomSD
/gate/shielding/attachPhantomSD

/gate/sourceCube/attachPhantomSD

# The coil and the cradle attach their own PhantomSD (in coil_bed_real/*.mac).



########################### PHYSICS #########################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# Physics lists: the interactions of the positrons, electrons and photons with the materials.


/gate/physics/addPhysicsList empenelope

/gate/physics/addProcess RadioactiveDecay
/gate/physics/addAtomDeexcitation
# empenelope already includes positron annihilation. The separate GATE PositronAnnihilation process stays
# off, because it would add annihilation a second time.
# With this GATE 8.2 / Geant4 10.5 Penelope model, annihilation at rest gives exactly opposite photons.
# The Na-22 decay and the positron transport (range) are modelled, but the acollinearity of the photons
# is not.
#/gate/physics/addProcess PositronAnnihilation

# Production cuts. A production cut decides which secondaries are created; it is not a distance at which the
# positron is stopped. Daughter volumes take the cuts of their region.
/gate/physics/Gamma/SetCutInRegion          world 0.1 mm
/gate/physics/Electron/SetCutInRegion       world 0.1 mm
/gate/physics/Positron/SetCutInRegion       world 0.1 mm
/gate/physics/Gamma/SetCutInRegion          sourceCube   0.01 mm
/gate/physics/Electron/SetCutInRegion       sourceCube   0.01 mm
/gate/physics/Positron/SetCutInRegion       sourceCube   0.01 mm

# Short positron steps in the acrylic source cube, because of the 9.4 T field.
/gate/physics/ActivateStepLimiter          e+
/gate/physics/SetMaxStepSizeInRegion        sourceCube   0.05 mm




####################### INITIALIZATION ######################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# All volumes (scanner, coil, cradle, source cube) must be defined before the initialization. The radioactive
# sources are defined after it.

/gate/run/initialize

# Geant4 overlap check of the geometry before any events are simulated. The log shows 'OK!' for every
# volume that does not overlap. Shared surfaces are fine; overlaps are not.
/geometry/test/verbosity true
/geometry/test/resolution 10000
/geometry/test/tolerance 0. mm
/geometry/test/run



########################## SOURCE ##########################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\

# NEMA NU 4-2008 section 5.3.3 (same source as sections 3.3.2/3.3.3) and Kuntner et al. 2025: Na-22 point
# source, 0.3 mm diameter, embedded in a 10 mm acrylic cube (sourceCube above), activity from SETUP
# (127.075 kBq Quad86, 131.126 kBq Quad35), 60 s per position.
# The source is Na-22 ions at rest followed by radioactive decay: GATE/Geant4 produce the beta+ (90 %) and
# EC branches, the positron spectrum, the positron transport in the 9.4 T field and the 1274.5 keV gamma.
# Activity = nuclear decays per second, not photon pairs per second: do not multiply it by the positron
# branching ratio. The half-life is fixed at 2.6019 y; decay during a 60 s run is negligible anyway.
# The source is attached to sourceCube: its position follows {sourceX} {sourceY} {sourceZ}.

/gate/source/addSource Na22PointSource
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setActivity {activity} becquerel	# set in SETUP

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setType            	gps
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/particle       	ion
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/ion       		11 22 0 0	# Z, A, charge, excitation energy.
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/monoenergy     	0. eV	# stationary parent ion; this is not the positron energy
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setForcedUnstableFlag 	true
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setForcedHalfLife 	82056672 s	# 2.6019 y

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/type 		Volume
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/shape 		Sphere
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/radius         0.15 mm	# 0.3 mm diameter (NEMA: <= 0.3 mm in all directions)
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/centre 		0. 0. 0. mm	# centre of sourceCube

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/angtype 		iso

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/attachTo           sourceCube	# coordinates local to the acrylic cube

# Defined first, so the Na-22 source gets sourceID 0 and Lu-176 gets sourceID 1
# (sens_analysis.py --source-id 0 selects the Na-22 events).



########################## Intrinsic Activity Source ##########################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\

# Intrinsic Lu-176 activity of the LYSO crystals, 152 kBq in all (calculated, not measured).
# sens_run.py --no-lu176 switches this source off. All results in the paper were run with it off.

/gate/source/addSource Lu176CrystalSource
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/setActivity 152000 becquerel

/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/setType gps
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/particle ion
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/ion 71 176 0 0
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/monoenergy 0. eV
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/setForcedUnstableFlag true
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/useDefaultHalfLife

# The decay points are sampled in world coordinates in a cylinder around the detector, and the confinement
# keeps only points inside the LYSO crystals. All crystal copies are named crystal_phys, so this one source
# covers all eight heads and all three rings. The cylinder is only a sampling region: it adds no material.
# Do not attach this source to a single crystal.

/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/type Volume
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/shape Cylinder
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/radius 75. mm
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/halfz 78. mm
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/centre 0. 0. 0. mm
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/angtype iso
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/confine crystal_phys



####################### VISUALIZATION ######################
#/////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The viewer commands are commented out, because the runs use GATE 8.2 without a display.
# To look at the model, use view_model.py (see the README).

#/vis/open 						OGLI		# uncomment and visualise using the command Gate --qt totalModelPET.mac
#/vis/viewer/zoom 					18		# zoom in/out
#/vis/viewer/set/viewpointThetaPhi 	60 60	# rotate
#/vis/drawVolume                        
#/vis/viewer/update
#/gate/geometry/rebuild



######################## DIGITIZER #########################
#/////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The digitizer turns the hits in the crystals into singles, as the detector electronics do. It is a chain
# of modules, each acting on the pulses of the one before. The output of the chain are the singles, which
# the coincidence sorter then pairs.
# Order here: adder, readout, energy blurring with QE, zero-energy filter, pile-up, deadtime, energy window.

### ADDER ####
# The adder sums the hits of one event in the same crystal into one pulse (energy-weighted position).

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert adder


### READOUT ###
# The readout sums the pulses of one detector block into one pulse (depth 3 = crystal).

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 		  	readout
/gate/digitizer/Singles/readout/setPolicy    TakeEnergyWinner	# keeps the energy-weighted position of each block
/gate/digitizer/Singles/readout/setDepth  	3 

# Depth 3 is the crystal in this PETscanner hierarchy: 24 monolithic blocks. The position of a pulse is the
# energy-weighted centroid of its interactions. The model does not simulate the light sharing on the SiPMs,
# a detector position blur, a depth-of-interaction estimate or the pixel grid of the scanner.


### ENERGY RESOLUTION ###
# Only one energy-resolution stage is used: crystalblurring below. The general blurring module stays
# switched off; do not switch on both.

#/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				    		blurring
#/gate/digitizer/Singles/blurring/setResolution	    		0.15
#/gate/digitizer/Singles/blurring/setEnergyOfReference 	511. keV


### CRYSTAL RESOLUTION ###
# crystalblurring: one fixed energy resolution (FWHM at 511 keV, scaled with sqrt(E) as GATE 8.2 does) and
# the QE. Min = Max, so no new resolution is drawn for each pulse.
# In this GATE module the QE is an acceptance per pulse: a rejected pulse gets zero energy. It is not an
# optical photon detection efficiency; no optical photons are transported. The QE of 0.90 is a model
# parameter, not a measured value.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert							   	crystalblurring
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalResolutionMin 	   	0.18
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalResolutionMax 		0.18
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalQE 			   	0.90
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalEnergyOfReference 	511. keV


# Remove the pulses rejected by the QE (zero energy) before pile-up and deadtime, so they cannot act there.
# The 0.001 keV threshold is only this zero filter, not the energy window of the acquisition.
/gate/digitizer/Singles/name                    zeroEnergyFilter
/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert                  thresholder
/gate/digitizer/Singles/zeroEnergyFilter/setThreshold 0.001 keV


### PILE-UP ###
# Pile-up: pulses in the same block (depth 3) within 100 ns are summed into one. The 100 ns is an
# electronics parameter, not just the LYSO decay time. Pulses that the final energy window rejects can still
# cause pile-up and deadtime. A piled-up pulse keeps the position and event label of one of its pulses.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert		 pileup
/gate/digitizer/Singles/pileup/setDepth  3
/gate/digitizer/Singles/pileup/setPileup 100 ns


### DEADTIME ###
# Two deadtime stages for each of the 24 blocks: 200 ns paralysable, then 600 ns nonparalysable.
# They act one after the other; they are not one deadtime of 800 ns.
# Both stages sit before the final energy window.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				deadtime
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime/setDeadTime 	200. ns
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime/setMode 		paralysable
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime/chooseDTVolume	crystal

/gate/digitizer/Singles/name 					deadtime2
/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				deadtime
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime2/setDeadTime 	600. ns
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime2/setMode 		nonparalysable
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime2/chooseDTVolume 	crystal


### THRESHOLDER ###
# Energy window 255.5-766.5 keV, the 50 % window of the sensitivity measurement (Kuntner et al. 2025).
# The NECR model uses 255.55-766.5 keV and the spatial resolution model 357.7-664.3 keV.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				thresholder
/gate/digitizer/Singles/thresholder/setThreshold  255.5 keV
/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				upholder
/gate/digitizer/Singles/upholder/setUphold 		766.5 keV


### COINCIDENCE SORTER ###
# GATE opens a window of length W at the first single, so W = 7 ns accepts pairs with |t1 - t2| < 7 ns.
# The 7 ns coincidence window of the measurement is taken as this largest time difference, and the delayed
# sorter uses the same window. No timing blur is applied.

/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setInputName       Singles
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setDepth           1
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/minSectorDifference 3
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/allPulseOpenCoincGate false
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setPresortBufferSize 256

/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setWindow            7 ns	# pairs with |t1 - t2| < 7 ns (see above)
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/MultiplesPolicy    takeAllGoods	# chosen because it matched the measurements best
# Other policies I tried: killAll lowered the randoms; takeWinnerIfIsGood raised the trues and the randoms
# and changed the scatter slightly.

/gate/digitizer/name                            delay
/gate/digitizer/insert                          coincidenceSorter
# Delayed window for the randoms estimate, offset by 10 microseconds. An offset of 200 ns would lie within
# the 600 ns deadtime and could tie the delayed window to the deadtime of the prompt partner.
/gate/digitizer/delay/setOffset                 10000. ns
/gate/digitizer/delay/setWindow                 7 ns				# same window as the prompt sorter
/gate/digitizer/delay/setInputName              Singles
/gate/digitizer/delay/setDepth                  1
/gate/digitizer/delay/minSectorDifference       3
/gate/digitizer/delay/allPulseOpenCoincGate     false
/gate/digitizer/delay/setPresortBufferSize      256
/gate/digitizer/delay/MultiplesPolicy           takeAllGoods

# Prompt and delayed sorters use the same multiples policy, set explicitly. The delayed sorter of GATE only
# pairs singles with the first single of its window, so equal settings do not guarantee an unbiased randoms
# estimate at high count rates.
# The GATE 8.2 sorter does not empty its last presort buffer at the end of a run, so very short runs (smoke
# tests) lose a few coincidences at the end. Do not use them to judge count rates.



######################### OUTPUT ###########################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# Output: one ROOT file per run, with the name set in RUN SETTINGS (e.g. output/Sens_z0.root).

/gate/output/root/enable
/gate/output/root/setFileName {output}				# set in RUN SETTINGS, e.g. output/Sens_z0
/gate/output/root/setRootHitFlag 			0		# 0 = do not store the Hits, 1 = store them
/gate/output/root/setRootSinglesFlag 		0		# 0 = do not store the Singles, 1 = store them
/gate/output/root/setRootCoincidencesFlag 	1		# prompt coincidences, read by sens_analysis.py
/gate/output/root/setRootdelayFlag 		1		# delayed coincidences, read by sens_analysis.py



################ RANDOM NUMBER GENERATOR ###################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

# Engines available: JamesRandom, Ranlux64, MersenneTwister
/gate/random/setEngineName JamesRandom
/gate/random/setEngineSeed {seed}	# set in RUN SETTINGS; different for every position
/gate/random/verbose 1

#/gate/random/setEngineSeed default
#/gate/random/resetEngineFrom fileName
#/gate/random/setEngineSeed default
#/gate/random/setEngineSeed auto



#################### SIMULATION TIME #######################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

/gate/application/setTimeSlice {duration} s	# one slice covering the whole acquisition
/gate/application/setTimeStart 0.0 s
/gate/application/setTimeStop  {duration} s

# In the measurement (Kuntner et al. 2025) each position took 60 s at 127-131 kBq (about 7.7 million Na-22
# decays). The duration is set in RUN SETTINGS at the top of this file (sens_run.py changes it).
# Every position needs its own random seed: the same seed with the same settings gives the same events.



####################
# Start simulation #
####################

/gate/application/startDAQ

#
